{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfSZfR5MY5VJAC7LrZkVG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 11 is done, marked `- [x]`, and PROGRESS.md carries the iteration 13 entry with the count at 11 of 14.\n\n**The answer to the item's question.** The ridge is a condition on one parameter across m/n, and that parameter is not the mean degree. The hardness peak sits where the excess of the product cover, `(n_ones − m)/n`, is about 2 to 2.4 at every ratio from m = 2n down to m = n/8. In customers-per-product terms the ridge is at `1 + 2.4·n/m`, which is why §11 saw \"three\" at m = n.\n\n**What decided it.**\n- The m = n/2 cells reject the mean degree. Calibrated at m = n, it predicts the half-ratio ridge at 4.7 customers per product. The measured peaks are 5.2 to 5.6, and every fixed-generator bootstrap interval excludes 4.7.\n- I generated the cells the campaign lacked, m = n/4 at n = 50, 60, 75 and m = n/8 at 75, in 82 seconds on 16 workers. There the gap opens: degree predicts 6.4 and 8.9 against measured 9.2 to 10.6 and 20.7. The excess predicts 10.7 to 11.1 and 21.2.\n- Across fifteen candidates the excess has the best constancy at the peaks, with mean degree a factor of 9.5 apart across ratios. `optimum/n` and `treewidth/n` match at n/2 and n/4 but fail at n/8 and are outputs of the instance, not parameters.\n\n**Two qualifications stated in §25.** The excess is an incidence-graph quantity, not a MOSP-graph invariant: its maximal-clique analogue is not constant, and no graph invariant tested is within a factor of 1.9 across ratios. The literature's transition, the giant component at branching factor 1 where treewidth and pathwidth become linear, is where the optimum becomes linear in n and lies 1.5 to 6 times below the ridge in density. The ridge's height, unlike its location, is set by m, growing one to one and a half decades per doubling of the products.\n\n**Deliverables.** `learning/ridge_theory.py` with run, cliques and tables stages, §25 of `reports/ml_nature.md`, `reports/ridge_theory_tables.md`, the overlay figure, `results_ratio.csv` with a verified manifest, and 9 new tests. Full suite: 1,021 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed in 82 seconds. Nothing written to `solutions/`, no solver default changed, nothing committed.\n\n**Process notes for the driver.** A maximal-clique stage stalled on two near-complete graphs and was killed by parent PID, then rerun with a density cutoff and a clique cap. The run wrote instance files that I removed after the manifest verified. The session used about 20 minutes of its 3-hour cap."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1043,"cache_read_input_tokens":224283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1043,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":803,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd5da557-da90-4a71-a6f8-1aa269bc8106","uuid":"66eb7595-1824-40fb-9e29-067fcde967ac","timestamp":"2026-09-26T18:58:51.625Z","request_id":"req_011CfSZfMuqsTjDaAwE2bjfS"}
